udns 0.5: stop configure from EXECUTING freshly linked probe binaries

`configure.lib` in 0.5 insists on running the executable it just linked:

  ac_prog_c_compiler_v()  (line 128)
      if ac_run $CC -o conftest conftest.c && ac_run ./conftest; then
                                                 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  ac_compile_run()        (line 227)
      ac_link "$@" && ac_run ./conftest

That is a native-build assumption.  udns is cross compiled for
aarch64-OHOS here, and the OHOS/HNP CI worker cannot exec the
transient AArch64 `./conftest` out of the source directory.  The
compile+link itself succeeds, but the run step fails, so configure
reports

  checking for C compiler... no
  configure: fatal: $CC (/data/service/hnp/bin/clang) is not a working compiler

and the build dies before a single object file is produced.

Upstream removed exactly these two exec steps in 0.6, replacing
`ac_compile_run` with the link-only `ac_link`:

  -    if ac_run $CC -o conftest conftest.c && ac_run ./conftest; then
  +    if ac_run $CC -o conftest conftest.c; then
  ...
  -     ac_compile_run <<EOF
  +     ac_link <<EOF

This patch backports that upstream change to 0.5.  Only the
"can the compiler produce a working executable" run steps are dropped;
every compile/link probe (including the inet_pton()/inet_ntop() one
fixed by patches/0001) is untouched, so feature detection such as
HAVE_INET_PTON_NTOP is unaffected.  A cross build may legitimately be
unable to exec its own target binaries, so this restores the standard
autoconf behaviour of "compile and link" rather than "compile, link and
run".

--- a/configure.lib	2011-05-23 16:09:15.000000000 +0800
+++ b/configure.lib	2026-10-08 00:00:00.000000000 +0800
@@ -125,7 +125,7 @@
   echo 'int main(int argc, char **argv) { return 0; }' >conftest.c
 
   if [ -n "$CC" ]; then
-    if ac_run $CC -o conftest conftest.c && ac_run ./conftest; then
+    if ac_run $CC -o conftest conftest.c; then
       ac_result "\$CC ($CC)"
     else
       ac_result no
@@ -133,7 +133,7 @@
     fi
   else
     for cc in gcc cc ; do
-      if ac_run $cc -o conftest conftest.c && ac_run ./conftest; then
+      if ac_run $cc -o conftest conftest.c; then
         ac_result "$cc"
         CC=$cc
         break
@@ -163,7 +163,7 @@
   if [ -n "$LIBS" ]; then ccld="$ccld $LIBS"; fi
   if ac_yesno "whenever the C compiler ($ccld)
            can produce executables" \
-     ac_compile_run <<EOF
+     ac_link <<EOF
 int main() { return 0; }
 EOF
   then :
